1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,329
Welcome to chapter fifteen of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,329 --> 00:00:18,765
We stop training and start building: a chat loop with memory, a retrieval augmented generation system with citations, a first look at tool calling, and simple automatic evaluation.

3
00:00:18,765 --> 00:00:28,921
I ran the whole pipeline against a real Llama 3.2 3B on my own machine, and I also found that the calculator tool's sandbox isn't a sandbox at all.

4
00:00:29,771 --> 00:00:33,081
We've changed weights and we've changed prompts.

5
00:00:33,081 --> 00:00:38,321
Today we build an application around the model: memory, documents and tools.

6
00:00:38,321 --> 00:00:43,492
Then chapters sixteen and seventeen make it production ready and deploy it.

7
00:00:43,492 --> 00:00:47,009
The notebook uses Ollama and sentence transformers.

8
00:00:47,859 --> 00:00:48,560
The plan.

9
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:49,963
The mindset shift.

10
00:00:49,963 --> 00:00:52,379
The three application patterns.

11
00:00:52,379 --> 00:00:53,314
A chat loop.

12
00:00:53,314 --> 00:00:54,717
RAG, step by step.

13
00:00:54,717 --> 00:00:56,899
Tool calling and its safety.

14
00:00:56,899 --> 00:00:59,548
Evaluation with my real model run.

15
00:00:59,548 --> 00:01:01,107
And production tips.

16
00:01:01,957 --> 00:01:02,701
Key terms.

17
00:01:02,701 --> 00:01:05,232
RAG means retrieve, then generate.

18
00:01:05,232 --> 00:01:09,326
A sentence embedding is one vector for a whole passage.

19
00:01:09,326 --> 00:01:11,261
Chunking splits documents.

20
00:01:11,261 --> 00:01:14,908
The context window is how much the model can see.

21
00:01:14,908 --> 00:01:19,375
Tool calling lets the model ask your code to run a function.

22
00:01:19,375 --> 00:01:22,501
And a citation ties a claim to its source.

23
00:01:23,351 --> 00:01:24,060
Part one.

24
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:25,399
A change of role.

25
00:01:26,249 --> 00:01:29,976
Nearly every training skill has an application twin.

26
00:01:29,976 --> 00:01:33,130
Token embeddings become sentence embeddings.

27
00:01:33,130 --> 00:01:36,068
Chunking data becomes chunking documents.

28
00:01:36,068 --> 00:01:38,362
Data quality becomes validation.

29
00:01:38,362 --> 00:01:40,655
Seeds and logs become debugging.

30
00:01:40,655 --> 00:01:42,376
Loss becomes evaluation.

31
00:01:42,376 --> 00:01:48,110
One correction: the book says embeddings and cosine similarity were chapter six.

32
00:01:48,110 --> 00:01:49,758
They were chapter nine.

33
00:01:50,608 --> 00:01:54,754
Three patterns, each adding capability and complexity.

34
00:01:54,754 --> 00:01:58,286
Pure prompting uses only what the model knows.

35
00:01:58,286 --> 00:02:01,127
RAG adds documents it has never seen.

36
00:02:01,127 --> 00:02:04,581
Tools let it fetch live data or take actions.

37
00:02:05,432 --> 00:02:06,413
Start simple.

38
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:07,395
Prompt first.

39
00:02:07,395 --> 00:02:10,794
Add retrieval when the model lacks knowledge.

40
00:02:10,794 --> 00:02:14,722
Add tools when it needs to act or calculate exactly.

41
00:02:14,722 --> 00:02:18,424
This chapter goes deep on RAG and previews tools.

42
00:02:19,274 --> 00:02:19,941
Part two.

43
00:02:19,941 --> 00:02:20,980
The chat loop.

44
00:02:21,830 --> 00:02:22,688
A chat loop.

45
00:02:22,688 --> 00:02:28,049
Read input, append it to history, send the whole history, append the reply.

46
00:02:28,049 --> 00:02:34,410
The model has no memory of its own; memory is just resending the conversation every turn.

47
00:02:34,410 --> 00:02:37,126
Which means the history grows forever.

48
00:02:37,976 --> 00:02:42,164
Every model has a context window covering prompt plus reply.

49
00:02:42,164 --> 00:02:45,654
The book says Llama 3.2's is about eight thousand.

50
00:02:45,654 --> 00:02:49,982
The model actually supports a hundred and thirty one thousand.

51
00:02:49,982 --> 00:02:58,149
But Ollama runs it with a much smaller window by default; on my machine the server ran with four thousand ninety six.

52
00:02:58,149 --> 00:03:02,616
So the practical limit is whatever you configure, using num ctx.

53
00:03:03,466 --> 00:03:11,392
The ChatSession class keeps at most twenty messages, dropping the oldest and keeping user and assistant turns paired.

54
00:03:11,392 --> 00:03:15,320
It counts messages, not tokens, which is simple but rough.

55
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:19,317
And leave about twenty percent of the window for the reply.

56
00:03:20,167 --> 00:03:28,187
The logging version calls count history tokens and count tokens, but neither method exists anywhere, so it crashes.

57
00:03:28,187 --> 00:03:36,487
Use a tokenizer, or read Ollama's prompt eval count and eval count from the response, which are the real token numbers.

58
00:03:37,337 --> 00:03:38,041
Part three.

59
00:03:38,041 --> 00:03:39,641
The heart of the chapter.

60
00:03:40,491 --> 00:03:44,496
Models have frozen training data and finite context.

61
00:03:44,496 --> 00:03:54,742
RAG turns the test into an open book exam: a retriever finds the right pages, like a librarian, and the model reads them and answers.

62
00:03:55,592 --> 00:04:00,557
Here's RAG done by hand: paste the policy into the prompt, then ask.

63
00:04:00,557 --> 00:04:02,163
That's the whole idea.

64
00:04:02,163 --> 00:04:06,472
The engineering is in finding the right text automatically.

65
00:04:07,322 --> 00:04:18,965
A sentence embedding model turns a whole passage into one vector, here three hundred and eighty four numbers, trained so that passages with similar meaning land close together.

66
00:04:18,965 --> 00:04:24,986
I ran the book's example: the cosine similarity is point six five seven, matching the book.

67
00:04:25,836 --> 00:04:28,044
Why not reuse our own embeddings?

68
00:04:28,044 --> 00:04:31,926
Token embeddings are trained for predicting the next word.

69
00:04:31,926 --> 00:04:35,340
Sentence encoders are trained for matching meaning.

70
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:41,430
Different objectives give different geometry, so use a purpose built encoder for retrieval.

71
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:52,202
Chunking splits text into windows of two hundred words that overlap by fifty, so a fact sitting on a boundary appears whole in at least one chunk.

72
00:04:52,202 --> 00:04:56,824
Too small and chunks lack context; too big and retrieval gets fuzzy.

73
00:04:56,824 --> 00:05:00,222
Two hundred to five hundred words is a good start.

74
00:05:01,072 --> 00:05:02,326
The full pipeline.

75
00:05:02,326 --> 00:05:05,115
Offline, chunk and embed your documents.

76
00:05:05,115 --> 00:05:14,597
Online, embed the question, find the most similar chunks, build a prompt with numbered sources, and let the model answer with citations.

77
00:05:15,447 --> 00:05:17,846
Retrieval is one matrix product.

78
00:05:17,846 --> 00:05:24,068
Normalise every vector to length one, and the dot product equals cosine similarity.

79
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:28,716
Sort, take the top k, and drop anything below a minimum score.

80
00:05:29,566 --> 00:05:40,150
The prompt numbers each source and names it, tells the model to cite by number, to use only these sources, and gives it permission to say the answer isn't there.

81
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:43,305
That last line is what keeps hallucination down.

82
00:05:44,155 --> 00:05:50,482
The notebook's knowledge base is three short policies: vacation, expenses and remote work.

83
00:05:50,482 --> 00:05:54,629
Each is under two hundred words, so each becomes one chunk.

84
00:05:55,479 --> 00:05:56,305
My real run.

85
00:05:56,305 --> 00:06:08,203
Retrieval works well on in-scope questions: the vacation policy scores point seven nine, the remote work policy point four two, and the answers are correct and mostly cited.

86
00:06:08,203 --> 00:06:13,293
The interesting failure is the out of scope question, the meaning of life.

87
00:06:13,293 --> 00:06:21,546
Nothing passed the minimum score, so the book's fallback prompt was sent, which offers to answer from general knowledge.

88
00:06:21,546 --> 00:06:27,943
The model did exactly that, wrote about Aristotle, and cited a source one that doesn't exist.

89
00:06:28,793 --> 00:06:33,325
Scoring with the book's evaluation set gives five out of eight traits.

90
00:06:33,325 --> 00:06:35,657
The vacation case passes everything.

91
00:06:35,657 --> 00:06:39,477
The expense case is correct but forgot the citation marker.

92
00:06:39,477 --> 00:06:45,887
The out of scope case fails both checks: it didn't refuse, and it cited a source that wasn't there.

93
00:06:45,887 --> 00:06:50,938
The fix is simple: if retrieval comes back empty, don't call the model at all.

94
00:06:50,938 --> 00:06:54,500
Return a fixed reply saying it isn't in your documents.

95
00:06:55,349 --> 00:06:56,243
Part four.

96
00:06:56,243 --> 00:06:56,779
Tools.

97
00:06:57,629 --> 00:06:59,960
Tool calling is a two turn dance.

98
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:01,584
You describe the tools.

99
00:07:01,584 --> 00:07:05,257
The model writes a tool request in an agreed format.

100
00:07:05,257 --> 00:07:12,744
Your code parses it, runs the function, and sends the result back so the model can write the final answer.

101
00:07:12,744 --> 00:07:15,357
The model never runs anything itself.

102
00:07:16,207 --> 00:07:21,620
Two tools: a calculator built on eval with empty builtins, and a date tool.

103
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:25,807
A regular expression pulls out the tool name and argument.

104
00:07:26,657 --> 00:07:27,310
Important.

105
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:32,076
The book says eval with empty builtins prevents arbitrary code execution.

106
00:07:32,076 --> 00:07:32,794
It doesn't.

107
00:07:32,794 --> 00:07:36,581
From any Python object you can walk to every loaded class.

108
00:07:36,581 --> 00:07:45,331
In my test, a single calculator expression reached the os module and printed the working directory, which means it could run anything.

109
00:07:45,331 --> 00:07:50,489
The book does mention simpleeval for production, but treat this code as unsafe.

110
00:07:50,489 --> 00:07:54,667
Parse with the ast module and allow only numbers and arithmetic.

111
00:07:55,517 --> 00:07:57,591
Tool calling, on my real run.

112
00:07:57,591 --> 00:08:07,817
For fifteen percent of eight four seven, the model just wrote calculate fifteen percent of eight four seven, with no tool tags, so nothing ran.

113
00:08:07,817 --> 00:08:11,035
For the date, it invented its own tag format.

114
00:08:11,035 --> 00:08:15,612
Only the capital of France went well: it correctly used no tool.

115
00:08:15,612 --> 00:08:17,757
So zero out of two tool calls.

116
00:08:17,757 --> 00:08:23,478
A three billion parameter model doesn't reliably follow an invented text format.

117
00:08:23,478 --> 00:08:30,199
Use the runtime's native tool calling, show the format with an example, or use a bigger model.

118
00:08:31,049 --> 00:08:31,949
Tool safety.

119
00:08:31,949 --> 00:08:33,149
Whitelist names.

120
00:08:33,149 --> 00:08:37,949
Validate arguments and never eval anything a user can influence.

121
00:08:37,949 --> 00:08:43,199
Start with read only tools and add write tools only with confirmation.

122
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:44,324
Log every call.

123
00:08:44,324 --> 00:08:50,249
A tool that can send email, delete files or spend money is a security boundary.

124
00:08:51,099 --> 00:08:51,791
Part five.

125
00:08:51,791 --> 00:08:53,382
Testing and production.

126
00:08:54,232 --> 00:08:56,576
LLM answers vary, so test traits.

127
00:08:56,576 --> 00:09:02,540
Does it contain the key words, cite a source, refuse when it should, and say enough?

128
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:09,357
The checker uses a phrase list for refusals, which misses natural wordings like I couldn't find.

129
00:09:10,207 --> 00:09:11,767
Temperature by task.

130
00:09:11,767 --> 00:09:16,367
Low for RAG, zero for tool calls, higher for creative work.

131
00:09:16,367 --> 00:09:28,063
The book's snippet here uses an OpenAI client with GPT three point five that the chapter never sets up; with Ollama you just pass temperature to chat.

132
00:09:28,913 --> 00:09:30,327
Two production tips.

133
00:09:30,327 --> 00:09:35,414
Stream tokens as they arrive; users feel it's faster even when it isn't.

134
00:09:35,414 --> 00:09:41,137
And cache embeddings keyed by a hash of the text, so you only embed what changed.

135
00:09:41,987 --> 00:09:44,751
When it misbehaves, go layer by layer.

136
00:09:44,751 --> 00:09:46,859
Input: what exactly was sent.

137
00:09:46,859 --> 00:09:49,550
Retrieval: which chunks, what scores.

138
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:52,094
Generation: temperature and prompt.

139
00:09:52,094 --> 00:09:55,875
And reproducibility: can you replay the exact input?

140
00:09:56,725 --> 00:09:57,895
My notebook run.

141
00:09:57,895 --> 00:10:02,937
Memory works: asked what it just asked, the model answered correctly.

142
00:10:02,937 --> 00:10:09,660
Retrieval passes one chunk per question, with scores of point seven one and point five five.

143
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:11,999
The RAG answers cite source one.

144
00:10:11,999 --> 00:10:21,426
Tool calling did not work: the three billion parameter model ignored the tool format and replied with plain text, so no tool ran.

145
00:10:21,426 --> 00:10:29,611
Two calls timed out at the helper's two minute limit because my CPU was busy, so I reran those in my own script.

146
00:10:29,611 --> 00:10:30,853
Streaming worked.

147
00:10:31,703 --> 00:10:32,443
Takeaways.

148
00:10:32,443 --> 00:10:37,551
An application is a model plus memory, knowledge, actions and checks.

149
00:10:37,551 --> 00:10:43,325
RAG is chunk, embed, retrieve, cite, and retrieval quality decides everything.

150
00:10:43,325 --> 00:10:47,026
Your code runs the tools, so you own their safety.

151
00:10:47,026 --> 00:10:51,098
Evaluate traits automatically and read the answers too.

152
00:10:51,098 --> 00:10:55,170
And check the context limit your runtime actually uses.

153
00:10:56,019 --> 00:10:57,012
Short answers.

154
00:10:57,012 --> 00:10:59,492
RAG adds facts the model never saw.

155
00:10:59,492 --> 00:11:06,508
Token embeddings live inside the model; sentence embeddings represent whole passages for retrieval.

156
00:11:06,508 --> 00:11:08,635
Chunking keeps things focused.

157
00:11:08,635 --> 00:11:13,666
A minimum score plus permission to say not found handles bad retrieval.

158
00:11:13,666 --> 00:11:19,123
And whitelisting stops the model calling functions you never meant to expose.

159
00:11:19,973 --> 00:11:24,142
Evaluate accuracy with an eval set and by reading samples.

160
00:11:24,142 --> 00:11:31,402
Log request ids, lengths, which sources were retrieved with their scores, tokens, latency and errors.

161
00:11:31,402 --> 00:11:38,661
The checkpoint is a personal knowledge assistant over your own files, with a ten question evaluation.

162
00:11:39,511 --> 00:11:40,153
Practice.

163
00:11:40,153 --> 00:11:45,140
Add questions your documents can't answer and check the app admits it.

164
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:51,481
Replace the eval calculator with an ast based one and confirm the escape no longer works.

165
00:11:51,481 --> 00:11:54,189
And log real token counts from Ollama.

166
